Anthony,

 > re-provision [the TPM2]

I suspect you are looking for the TPM's TPM2_Clear() command. =

Unfortunately I am unable to recommend which TSS command to use to =

execute TPM2_Clear().

You may or may not be interested in this simplified architectural =

explanation:

  *  =C2=A0=C2=A0=C2=A0 the (electrical) reset signal instructs the TPM2 th=
at its host
    platform has been _reset_, to make the TPM reset its PCRs (because
    PCRS are essentially a history of the execution of software on the
    platform since reset).
  *  =C2=A0=C2=A0=C2=A0 a (software) TPM2_Clear() command instructs the TPM=
2 to revert
    to its default _state_ (in other words, remove personalisation of
    TPM state by the previous TPM "owner").

Apologies if this type of information shouldn't be posted to this reflector.

Regards,

G.Proudler

----------------------------

On 02/06/2021 08:31, Peter.Huewe(a)infineon.com wrote:
>
> Hi,
>
> Pressing the reset (or doing it via GPIO if the jumper is in the other =

> position), does only send an =E2=80=98electrical=E2=80=99 reset to the TP=
M.
>
> This does NOT *clear* the tpm ,( and also of course does not =

> de-provision the fapi.)
>
> A reset only resets the volatile states.
>
>
> You can verify that the reset is working, by using any regular esapi =

> tool (tpm2_xxxx) and you will get an error RC_INITIALIZE 0x100.
>
> Then you would need to send a tpm2_startup command.
>
> FAPI tools (tss2_xxxx) do send the startup automatically.
>
> Thanks,
> Peter
